---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const BadBrief = `Prompt the user for the subtotal.
Prompt the user for the tip percentage.
Multiply the subtotal by the percentage divided by 100.
Print the tip and the total.`;

const RealBrief = `My bowling league splits the bill evenly at the end
of the night, except the league covers shoe rental for
everyone. Tell me what each person owes.`;

const Extracted = `Requirements for "bowling league"

INPUT
  - total bill
  - number of people
  - cost of shoe rental
CALCULATE
  - bill minus shoe rental
  - that amount divided by the number of people
OUTPUT
  - what each person owes

UNCLEAR - I had to decide:
  - Is the shoe rental one price for everyone, or per person?
    I assumed per person.`;

const challenges = [
  { slug: "cc-name-card", title: "Name Card", stars: 1, after: "1.2" },
  { slug: "cc-receipt-line", title: "Receipt Line", stars: 1, after: "1.3" },
  { slug: "cc-time-split", title: "Time Split", stars: 1, after: "1.4" },
  { slug: "cc-pizza-split", title: "Pizza Split", stars: 1, after: "1.4" },
  { slug: "cc-trip-cost", title: "Trip Cost", stars: 1, after: "1.7" },
  { slug: "cc-locker-combo", title: "Locker Combo", stars: 1, after: "1.7" },
  { slug: "cc-temperature", title: "Temperature", stars: 1, after: "1.8" },
  { slug: "cc-score-average", title: "Score Average", stars: 2, after: "1.8" },
  { slug: "cc-screen-time", title: "Screen Time", stars: 2, after: "1.8" },
];
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge Set 1</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Complete any 5 of the 9 below.</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Set Is For</h2>

          <p>
            Every challenge here uses only what you already know: printing,
            variables, arithmetic, input, and casting. None of them is hard.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The difficulty is not the point. The reading is.</b> Each
            challenge describes a problem the way a person would actually
            describe it &mdash; vaguely, out of order, and with details missing.
            Your job is to turn that into a list of things your program has to
            do, and then write it.
          </p>

          <p>
            That skill has a name on this course: <b>gathering requirements</b>.
            It is the first thing you will do on every project this year, and it
            is worth points on the AP exam &mdash; one of the written response
            questions asks you to identify who your program is for and explain
            how your design serves them.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="how-to-read-a-brief">
          <h2>How to Read a Brief</h2>

          <p>
            Here is the difference between a set of instructions and a real
            request. Instructions look like this:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={BadBrief}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Instructions — not what you will get"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">A real request looks like this:</p>

          <Code
            code={RealBrief}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="A Brief"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            The second one never says "prompt," never says "divide," and never
            mentions a variable. It also leaves something genuinely unclear. You
            work that out first, on paper, before you write any code:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Extracted}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Requirements"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Noticing what is unclear matters as much as listing what is
            clear.</b> Real requests are always missing something. Write down
            the gap, write down the decision you made, and move on &mdash; do
            not wait for permission.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="challenges">
          <h2>The Challenges</h2>

          <p>
            Pick <b>any five</b>. They unlock as you go &mdash; the activity
            each one follows is listed beside it.
          </p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              challenges.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-terminal text-sn-theme me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-4">
            The four unstarred by you are not extra credit and not a punishment
            for being slow. They are there so that a bad week costs you nothing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For <b>each</b> of the five you choose, submit two things:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Your requirements list</b> &mdash; written before you coded,
              in the shape shown above. Input, calculate, output, and anything
              you had to decide for yourself.
            </li>
            <li><b>Your program file.</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>The requirements list is not a formality and it is not graded on
            neatness.</b> It is graded on whether it exists and whether it
            matches what you built. A list written afterward to match finished
            code is easy to spot and defeats the purpose entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            This set is worth <b>10 points</b>, graded on completion. Five honest
            attempts earn all ten, whether or not every program works perfectly.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenges
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
